 "discovery_answer": "I found the agent's egress and registered it. This is what `nookku register` printed:\n\n```\nnookku: the egress answer is registered. The next start uses these parts:\n- model API anthropic: ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL -> the URL in ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL or the default (proxy)\n- MCP server catalog: http CATALOG_MCP_URL -> http://127.0.0.1:9310/mcp (proxy)\n- MCP server notes: stdio NOTES_MCP_COMMAND -> python3 notes_mcp.py (proxy)\n- database shop: postgres, DATABASE_URL (proxy)\n- database returns: sqlite, returns.db (driver trace)\n```\n\nThings to check:\n- **Model API:** the agent runs a Claude Agent SDK session (model `haiku`) and doesn't set a URL. So the test uses whatever is in `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` when it starts, or the default URL if that's empty.\n- **Not listed:** the `shop` MCP server holds the agent's own tools, `lookup_order` and `start_return`. It runs inside the agent's process, so it has no egress of its own. What those tools touch is covered by the two databases. The `notes.json` file is also left out because only the notes MCP server uses it, and that server's proxy records the tool call.\n- **SQLite driver trace:** I didn't change any code. `app.py:45-69` already uses the SQLite3 instrumentation, which records statement text, and sends spans with the OTLP exporter over HTTP. Nookku sets the `OTEL_*` variables during the test. Two limits:\n  - A driver trace records no rows from the answers.\n  - It can hide parameter values: the `?` placeholders in the `INSERT` may show up without their values.\n\nIf the parts are right, type `nookku start` within the next 30 minutes. If one is wrong, tell me and I'll correct it and register again.",
